<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Agona</h1><p class="st-meta">1500 CE – 1874 CE (approx.) · capital Nyakrom</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Akan kingdom of the central Gold Coast, known in 17th century European reports for being ruled by queens.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Agona was a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> state on the coast and hinterland between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> lands and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/accra">Accra</a>. It existed by the 16th century, with its political centre inland at Nyakrom and coastal towns that traded with European ships.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>European visitors in the 17th century recorded female rulers of Agona, reflecting the importance of royal women in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> politics. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> built Fort Patience at Apam on its coast in the 1690s. Agona traded gold, slaves and food with European companies, but was often subject to stronger neighbours such as <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/akwamu">Akwamu</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the 18th century Agona was drawn into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> sphere and the wars against the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>. It came under British rule with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a> in 1874, and survives as a traditional area in Ghana's Central Region.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1500 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">West Africa</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:89.55%;width:7.44%"></span></div><div class="st-spa